{"prompt": "We need discover one genuinely new, broad world-model security mechanism for an ICLR-scale paper. Current repository has official TD-MPC2 and MBRL-Lib/PETS-style ensemble carriers. Many candidates already failed: generic shared-model interference, replay/optimizer state, CEM covariance, temporal residual coupling, cross-candidate sampling coupling, branch observation splice, retrieval memory capture, action routing, terminal routing, private query leakage, and non-normal dynamics. We have not yet tested a native mixture-of-world-models or prototype-router carrier.

Evaluate this proposed family: “truthful on-manifold interactions can hijack a test-time world-model router rather than any model weights; by moving the trajectory prototype/assignment boundary, an attacker makes a victim context select an individually accurate but wrong expert. Every expert’s held-out one-step marginal and on-policy forecasts remain intact, yet the router’s closed-loop expert identity persists after attacker exit and causes task loss. A matched permutation/sham control should preserve coverage and model error but not the assignment boundary.”

Be very strict. If this is just retrieval poisoning or ordinary negative transfer, reject it. If it can be novel, specify a concrete released carrier and a two-minute gate using exact files/classes in the repository, including source/victim tasks, how to construct experts, what remains unchanged, metrics, and controls. Do not recommend a large GPU run until the gate is positive. Consider whether a minimal implementation in MBRL-Lib BasicEnsemble or PETS counts as native, and whether this is genuinely a world-model mechanism rather than a generic router attack."}
